---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ImageTool from '../components/ImageTool.tsx';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize Image to KB Online, Free | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Reduce a photo to the file size your form allows, in KB, right in your browser. Preview, compare sizes and download. The code here does not upload your image.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune image resizer', '/', description);
---
<Base pageType="home" title={title} description={description} path="/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">Resize an image to a size in KB</h1>
  <p class="mb-6">
    Choose a picture, pick the largest file size your form accepts, and SizeTune makes a smaller copy for you in your
    browser. You see a preview, the before and after sizes, and a download button once the result fits.
  </p>

  <ImageTool client:load defaultMaxKb={50} />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-3">
      SizeTune needs JavaScript, because the resizing happens inside your browser and not on a server. Turn JavaScript on
      and reload this page to use the tool. The explanation below is still readable without it.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>What the tool does</h2>
    <p>
      Many upload forms refuse a file that is even slightly over a limit written in kilobytes. A modern phone photo is
      usually several megabytes, so it has to shrink a lot before a form will take it. SizeTune does that shrinking
      without any software to install. You can drop a file onto the page or use the file picker, then set the maximum
      size with one of the presets (10, 20, 30, 50, 100 or 200 KB) or type your own number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some forms also set a minimum, for example a file that must be between 20 and 50 KB. You can enter a range
      instead of a single limit. The tool shows the original and the new file size, the pixel width and height before
      and after, and a before and after view so you can judge the picture yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>How a picture gets under the limit</h2>
    <p>
      There are two levers, and the tool lets you choose which one comes first. With quality-first, the pixel
      dimensions stay as they are and the compression is made stronger until the file fits. With dimensions-first, the
      picture is made smaller in pixels and the quality is kept higher. Quality-first suits a form that fixes the exact
      pixel size. Dimensions-first suits a form that only cares about the file size, because a smaller picture with
      cleaner detail often looks better than a large, heavily compressed one.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want a specific width or height, type it in. The aspect lock keeps the picture from being stretched. The
      output is a JPEG by default, which is the usual choice for photographs. PNG and WebP are offered when your browser
      is able to produce them. The tool can also be stopped part-way with the cancel button, and nothing is saved until
      you press download.
    </p>

    <h2>When a target cannot be reached</h2>
    <p>
      File size follows the amount of visual detail and the number of pixels. A large, busy photograph has a lot of
      both, so a very small limit forces either a strong drop in quality or a big drop in dimensions. At the other end,
      a very small and plain image, such as a tiny scan of a signature, may already be smaller than a minimum that your
      form sets, and no honest setting makes it larger in a useful way.
    </p>
    <p>
      In those cases SizeTune tells you what happened and shows the closest result it could produce, instead of
      presenting a file that misses the limit as a success. Exact sizes are never guaranteed. A result is judged by the
      number you see next to the file, so read it before you upload.
    </p>

    <h2>Pages for specific jobs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/resize-image-to-20kb/">Resize an image to 20 KB</a> opens the tool with a 20 KB maximum and explains
        how to get there with the least visible damage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/resize-signature/">Resize a signature</a> adds cropping and positioning for scanned or photographed
        signatures, and explains why a small signature can fall short of a minimum.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/photo-size-for-online-forms/">The photo size guide</a> explains pixels, kilobytes and file
        formats from the beginning.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      SizeTune does not know what any particular form requires. Always follow the instructions written on your own
      form or portal, because they can change from one application round to the next.
    </p>

    <h2>Your images stay in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The picture you choose is read and re-encoded by code running on your own device. SizeTune's code does not upload
      it, and this page loads no advertising or analytics scripts. Opening the page still sends the normal request that
      every website receives, which the hosting company handles. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> explains
      this in detail, and the <a href="/about/">about page</a> says who runs the site and how to reach them.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Is 20 KB the same as 20,000 bytes or 20,480 bytes?</h3>
    <p>
      Both conventions are in use. Some systems count a kilobyte as 1,000 bytes and others as 1,024. SizeTune aims for a
      result that is under the limit by the smaller measure, so it should pass whichever one the form uses. If you set
      a minimum, it aims above the larger measure for the same reason.
    </p>
    <h3>Will my picture look worse?</h3>
    <p>
      Usually a little, and sometimes a lot, depending on how small the limit is compared with the original. JPEG
      compression discards detail, and the tool looks for the highest quality that still fits. The preview lets you
      compare before you download.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my photo sent anywhere?</h3>
    <p>
      SizeTune's code keeps the image on your device and does not upload it. Browser extensions or device backup
      features that you have installed are outside the site's control.
    </p>
    <h3>Which pictures can I open?</h3>
    <p>
      Any image your browser can decode, which normally includes JPEG, PNG and WebP. Support for other types, such as
      HEIC photos from some phones, depends on the browser. If a file will not open, save or export it as a JPEG first.
    </p>
    <h3>Why did the tool say it could not reach my limit?</h3>
    <p>
      The limit is too small for that image at the chosen settings, or the minimum is too large for it. Try the
      dimensions-first option, allow a smaller width and height, choose a plainer picture, or raise the limit if your
      form permits it.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it work on a phone?</h3>
    <p>
      It runs in current mobile browsers. Very large photos use a lot of memory, so an older phone may be slow or may
      fail on a huge file. If that happens, try again with a smaller original.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it cost anything?</h3>
    <p>No. There is no account, no sign-up and no watermark added by SizeTune.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
